WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.260
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:05.760
VISHAL GOENKA: Thank
you for coming.

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:07.070
My name is Vishal Goenka.

00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:09.560
I'm a product manager at Google.

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:12.520
I'm responsible for
growing our Maps API

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:16.690
business in key verticals
by enabling our partners

00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:20.020
and our customers build
compelling businesses powered

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:22.600
by Google's Geo services.

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:27.670
Today it is my privilege
to talk to you about some

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:31.060
of the key
location-related challenges

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:34.630
that the on-demand
economy is facing.

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:43.200
Now, powered by smartphones,
a number of industries

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:46.960
have been disrupted
in the space.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:55.150
And they have generated new
user behaviors and expectations

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:56.860
in the process.

00:00:56.860 --> 00:01:00.100
Today what I will do is I will
examine the impact of some

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:03.250
of these new user expectations
and behaviors created

00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:08.320
by location-enabled
services, on-demand services,

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:15.040
and the impact of that on the
economics of these businesses,

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:19.690
and what opportunities lies
ahead of us as we go forward.

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:24.880
I'm going to focus
on two specific areas

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:30.005
of this economy-- the
local rides and delivery.

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:39.140
Let me start by asking
you a very simple question

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:41.090
to get a sense of the audience.

00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:45.905
How many of you here have used
a local ride service in the US?

00:01:49.391 --> 00:01:51.739
A delivery service
recently in the US

00:01:51.739 --> 00:01:53.780
where you've have had food
delivered or groceries

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:55.980
delivered?

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:57.420
What about outside the US?

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:00.420
What about Europe?

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:02.085
OK.

00:02:02.085 --> 00:02:02.585
Africa?

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:07.210
One.

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:11.240
How about Latin America?

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:14.260
Southeast Asia?

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:16.980
Australia?

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:19.290
So very few outside
of the United States

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:21.210
and possibly Europe.

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:25.700
Some of the things I'm going
to talk about apply more

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:28.920
in certain geographies
than others,

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.240
but there is a common thread.

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:33.130
Let me begin first
with a personal story.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:38.500
About three to four
years ago, I was

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:41.980
at the Google office in New
York City for a meeting,

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:48.320
and I had to catch a flight back
at 7:00 PM to San Francisco.

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:50.300
I wrapped up my
meetings by 3 o'clock

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:54.820
and I headed down
to catch a taxi.

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:56.260
It was raining.

00:02:56.260 --> 00:02:59.770
And after waiting for
30 minutes in the rain,

00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:02.050
I finally decided
to take the train,

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:05.770
and I barely made it
to the airport on time.

00:03:05.770 --> 00:03:10.580
I later realized why I could
not get a cab that day.

00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:13.550
Because of the rain, the
cabbies had made their money

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:17.120
earlier in the day
because of higher demand

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:19.760
and called it a day.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:21.530
Now, there was no
way back then I

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:25.890
could monitor the
inventory of drivers

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:30.020
on the road in real time
and not being a local

00:03:30.020 --> 00:03:33.580
could have predicted that this
would be an expected pattern.

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:35.705
Apparently, the locals
could have predicted this

00:03:35.705 --> 00:03:37.330
because they have
faced it a few times.

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:43.800
Now, when I travel
to India, which

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:49.840
is where I was born and raised
and I still have extended

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:54.400
family there, if I
needed to move around,

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:58.810
I would hire a car for the
entire day with a driver.

00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:02.470
Because finding a cab where
you need it and when you need

00:04:02.470 --> 00:04:05.284
it was impossible.

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:14.890
Today, on the other hand, I
sit comfortably in my office,

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:18.670
maybe as I'm wrapping up my
last meeting for the day,

00:04:18.670 --> 00:04:22.960
or I'm at the terminal
of an airport getting

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:26.740
ready to pick up my bags, and I
can see how long it would take

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:29.530
for the next ride to arrive.

00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:31.462
I can schedule it ahead of time.

00:04:31.462 --> 00:04:32.920
And if it's raining,
I can actually

00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:37.660
show up just moments before
my ride actually arrives.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.220
In the last few
years, I've traveled

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:46.380
over half a dozen
countries and have

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:49.736
had very similar experiences
across all of those countries.

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:56.290
Let's switch gears
to ordering food.

00:04:56.290 --> 00:04:58.390
We normally eat out,
but sometimes when

00:04:58.390 --> 00:05:01.070
we bring food
home, it used to be

00:05:01.070 --> 00:05:04.130
that I would look in my
drawers for printed menus

00:05:04.130 --> 00:05:07.970
that I received in the mail
from various restaurants

00:05:07.970 --> 00:05:14.050
or looked up their menu
online, if they had one,

00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:16.290
and then I would call them.

00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:18.360
But then either the
menus were out of date,

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:21.660
the prices were
out of date, or I

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:25.170
had to plan a trip to go to
the restaurant, which limited

00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:27.600
my choices to only
restaurants that

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:32.210
were within the range of
my willingness to drive.

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:35.940
There simply weren't
enough delivery options

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:41.420
that would deliver unless
you had a really huge order.

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:46.730
Today I browse rich
menus on the phone.

00:05:46.730 --> 00:05:49.550
I can order all
sorts of cuisines--

00:05:49.550 --> 00:05:56.650
Italian, Indian, Chinese,
Vietnamese, Japanese--

00:05:56.650 --> 00:06:05.360
and I can be assured that they
will arrive when I need it to.

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:09.110
I can be at the
soccer game of my kids

00:06:09.110 --> 00:06:13.970
and order food knowing that by
the time the kids have returned

00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:19.320
and showered the food will be
at our doorstep ready to munch.

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:24.450
If my kids want cheese
pizza, and my wife

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:28.730
and I prefer something
else, perhaps Thai,

00:06:28.730 --> 00:06:31.680
we can place two separate
orders and have them

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:32.930
be delivered at the same time.

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:39.480
I don't know how many of you
were at Gayathri's session

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:43.800
yesterday, but if you were,
she spoke about location

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:47.260
as a force multiplier.

00:06:47.260 --> 00:06:52.240
And she gave examples of how
many businesses, including

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:55.660
companies in the local
rides and delivery space,

00:06:55.660 --> 00:07:00.130
have built amazing businesses
and created new user

00:07:00.130 --> 00:07:05.050
experiences by leveraging
Google's incredibly

00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:10.020
rich and complex understanding
of the real world.

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:12.360
Now, traditional
taxi drivers did not

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:15.720
need a map to navigate the
cities that they served.

00:07:25.982 --> 00:07:27.690
They had developed
their hippocampus area

00:07:27.690 --> 00:07:33.090
of their memory because
of the spatial knowledge

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:35.220
that they had to develop
in order to navigate

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:38.240
the world without maps.

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:41.150
Now, once maps got
digitized, some of them

00:07:41.150 --> 00:07:45.170
started switching and
using Maps as a tool

00:07:45.170 --> 00:07:49.280
when they needed to go
somewhere they didn't know.

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:53.180
However, today even
though I know the route

00:07:53.180 --> 00:07:57.110
that I commute every day
and I know my way back home,

00:07:57.110 --> 00:08:01.070
I still check Google
Maps every day.

00:08:01.070 --> 00:08:04.730
Because sometimes there
is a more efficient route.

00:08:04.730 --> 00:08:07.520
Maybe there is an accident
or an unfortunate incident

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:08.700
on the road.

00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:12.380
Or perhaps the 49ers
are playing nearby

00:08:12.380 --> 00:08:14.780
and the roads are jammed.

00:08:14.780 --> 00:08:18.350
And Google Maps is able
to tell me in real time

00:08:18.350 --> 00:08:23.010
an alternate route that is
more effective for my journey.

00:08:23.010 --> 00:08:28.160
If as some of you have tried
these ride sharing services

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:30.070
in other countries,
not just the US,

00:08:30.070 --> 00:08:35.976
you'd have probably seen the
familiar Google-powered maps

00:08:35.976 --> 00:08:37.559
driving many of these
companies today.

00:08:41.159 --> 00:08:43.080
We have a very rich
set of APIs that

00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:46.320
enable developers
and our partners

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:51.630
build these applications
across multiple platforms,

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:56.720
whether it's iOS, or Android,
a good old website using

00:08:56.720 --> 00:09:03.026
JavaScript, or web services to
enable access to our services

00:09:03.026 --> 00:09:03.900
through your servers.

00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:12.280
Today we have over 3 million
developers and over 10,000

00:09:12.280 --> 00:09:17.480
paying customers using our
APIs to build everything

00:09:17.480 --> 00:09:21.330
from visualizing
a point on a map,

00:09:21.330 --> 00:09:27.360
to routing for deliveries or
rides, or even programming

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:33.600
television remotes based on the
location that the device is at.

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:39.540
Yesterday, Vagabond was
here, and they gave us

00:09:39.540 --> 00:09:43.650
an example of how they
used Google's APIs

00:09:43.650 --> 00:09:46.170
to program automatic
vending machines

00:09:46.170 --> 00:09:52.520
and simplified the workflow for
their workforce significantly

00:09:52.520 --> 00:09:56.290
and saved a lot of
money in the process.

00:09:56.290 --> 00:10:01.480
Looking forward, we're
building on these capabilities

00:10:01.480 --> 00:10:04.930
and bringing together
all of our real time

00:10:04.930 --> 00:10:08.710
and historical
knowledge of the world

00:10:08.710 --> 00:10:11.440
and combining it with our
machine learning and cloud

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:16.680
computing to bring
new predictive powers

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:19.630
for our customers, bringing
new insights, as well as

00:10:19.630 --> 00:10:21.190
predictive capabilities.

00:10:21.190 --> 00:10:25.760
But in order to understand where
this takes us in the future,

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:28.920
let's focus on the user.

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:31.660
At Google, we strongly believe
that if you focus on the user,

00:10:31.660 --> 00:10:32.740
all things become clear.

00:10:35.490 --> 00:10:40.150
So speaking of local
rides and deliveries,

00:10:40.150 --> 00:10:44.650
reflecting back on the habits
that have changed for me--

00:10:44.650 --> 00:10:46.270
and I'm sure for many of you--

00:10:46.270 --> 00:10:49.140
the ease with which
these habits changed

00:10:49.140 --> 00:10:55.450
did not surprise me until I
stopped to think about it.

00:10:55.450 --> 00:10:58.390
My first experience with
these ride-sharing services

00:10:58.390 --> 00:11:00.280
was a magical one,
even though it took me

00:11:00.280 --> 00:11:03.430
a little bit of overcoming
the inertia to try them.

00:11:03.430 --> 00:11:08.480
It was such a magical
experience that I got hooked.

00:11:08.480 --> 00:11:12.710
Over the last 100-plus years
since Ford invented the Model-T

00:11:12.710 --> 00:11:15.410
and changed transportation
in a fundamental way,

00:11:15.410 --> 00:11:19.560
we have developed a number
of habits around mobility.

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:21.840
Let's take a quick walk
down the memory lane

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:23.250
and examine some
of these and how

00:11:23.250 --> 00:11:27.370
they have changed over time.

00:11:27.370 --> 00:11:29.950
In most country-- well,
in many countries,

00:11:29.950 --> 00:11:33.740
I wouldn't say most-- in many
countries around the world,

00:11:33.740 --> 00:11:37.920
car ownership is a very
common habit that many of us

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:43.106
grew up with, even though we
all understand that owning a car

00:11:43.106 --> 00:11:44.730
is not the most
economical decision you

00:11:44.730 --> 00:11:47.000
could make for mobility.

00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:51.410
Most of our cars spend most
of their time sitting idle.

00:11:51.410 --> 00:11:58.090
And that garage that we dedicate
a huge fraction of our house

00:11:58.090 --> 00:12:01.640
to store the car while it
is not being productive

00:12:01.640 --> 00:12:03.450
does cost a lot of money.

00:12:03.450 --> 00:12:06.720
And if you add the cost
of gas, maintenance,

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:09.560
the payments for
the car, insurance,

00:12:09.560 --> 00:12:11.180
it's not the most
economical decision.

00:12:11.180 --> 00:12:13.610
And on top of it, we
have developed the habit

00:12:13.610 --> 00:12:18.404
of spending hours behind
the steering wheel

00:12:18.404 --> 00:12:20.320
when we could have done
something more useful.

00:12:23.980 --> 00:12:31.580
When we were called upon to
hire a ride for a short drive,

00:12:31.580 --> 00:12:33.770
we have created a whole
institution and a protocol

00:12:33.770 --> 00:12:36.140
around this notion.

00:12:36.140 --> 00:12:39.290
So we call it taxis,
or cabs, or taxi cabs.

00:12:39.290 --> 00:12:42.590
Let's examine some of the
protocols we have developed.

00:12:42.590 --> 00:12:45.230
These cars are painted
differently so that we can

00:12:45.230 --> 00:12:48.260
recognize them from a distance.

00:12:48.260 --> 00:12:51.650
They have special signs on them
that you can read from afar

00:12:51.650 --> 00:12:55.280
and know whether you
can hire them or not.

00:12:55.280 --> 00:12:58.940
In airports, we have dedicated
signs and special queues--

00:12:58.940 --> 00:13:00.440
sometimes really long ones--

00:13:00.440 --> 00:13:04.670
and people there to
manage the queues.

00:13:04.670 --> 00:13:07.010
Hotels have a special
protocol and a special area

00:13:07.010 --> 00:13:09.830
for getting people
in and out of taxis.

00:13:09.830 --> 00:13:13.150
In many countries,
there are special lanes

00:13:13.150 --> 00:13:14.780
that taxis can
drive on, or they're

00:13:14.780 --> 00:13:17.130
allowed to drive in bus lanes.

00:13:17.130 --> 00:13:18.800
There are special,
designated areas

00:13:18.800 --> 00:13:22.310
where taxis can park that
nobody else can park,

00:13:22.310 --> 00:13:25.730
or where you would
go to find the cab.

00:13:25.730 --> 00:13:29.930
And last but not least,
there's a familiar hand gesture

00:13:29.930 --> 00:13:32.424
to hail a cab that
we have developed,

00:13:32.424 --> 00:13:34.340
and it may vary from one
culture to the other.

00:13:37.480 --> 00:13:43.130
Contrasting with the ride
sharing experience of today,

00:13:43.130 --> 00:13:45.770
we schedule our
rides from our phone

00:13:45.770 --> 00:13:46.900
regardless of where we are.

00:13:50.070 --> 00:13:52.230
Once we have scheduled
the ride, finding

00:13:52.230 --> 00:13:56.160
how to find the specific
car that is assigned to you

00:13:56.160 --> 00:13:58.260
requires a very new protocol.

00:13:58.260 --> 00:14:03.180
You expect to call the driver,
or the driver to call you,

00:14:03.180 --> 00:14:04.540
in many cases.

00:14:04.540 --> 00:14:08.240
Your car could be
a Toyota Corolla

00:14:08.240 --> 00:14:10.700
or a minivan
indistinguishable from any

00:14:10.700 --> 00:14:12.500
of the other cars on the road.

00:14:12.500 --> 00:14:15.680
And you really wish you had
eagle eyes to spot that license

00:14:15.680 --> 00:14:18.710
plate from a distance so
that you know it's your ride.

00:14:21.450 --> 00:14:26.380
Once the car arrives,
we have told ourselves

00:14:26.380 --> 00:14:28.990
it's OK if the driver does
not get out of the car

00:14:28.990 --> 00:14:30.707
to handle our bags for us.

00:14:30.707 --> 00:14:33.040
For all we know, he might be
a regular Joe just like you

00:14:33.040 --> 00:14:36.610
and me, or a student
doing this part time,

00:14:36.610 --> 00:14:37.870
or a person with a desk job.

00:14:41.120 --> 00:14:44.510
And then, once we arrive
at our destination,

00:14:44.510 --> 00:14:46.640
we have accustomed
ourselves to not

00:14:46.640 --> 00:14:50.900
bother about ensuring that we
have enough cash in our pocket,

00:14:50.900 --> 00:14:54.860
or whether or not the driver is
going to accept a credit card.

00:14:54.860 --> 00:14:57.350
So a number of these
habits, small ones,

00:14:57.350 --> 00:15:01.070
have changed as a result
of this transformation.

00:15:01.070 --> 00:15:04.109
The first time I ordered
a ride for somebody else,

00:15:04.109 --> 00:15:05.150
it was even more magical.

00:15:07.670 --> 00:15:10.070
A cousin of mine
was visiting us,

00:15:10.070 --> 00:15:12.930
and she was going to
spend the weekend with us.

00:15:12.930 --> 00:15:19.040
She landed in San Jose, but I
had an unavoidable conflict,

00:15:19.040 --> 00:15:21.620
and I could not go
there to pick her up.

00:15:21.620 --> 00:15:25.250
So I ordered a ride
to show up as soon

00:15:25.250 --> 00:15:26.930
as she arrived at the airport.

00:15:26.930 --> 00:15:30.860
I took a screenshot of my
app, and I texted it to her,

00:15:30.860 --> 00:15:34.490
and she arrived home just
as when I did from work.

00:15:34.490 --> 00:15:36.590
It was, again, another
magical experience

00:15:36.590 --> 00:15:40.110
enabled by the service.

00:15:40.110 --> 00:15:42.060
And of course,
now when I travel,

00:15:42.060 --> 00:15:44.520
I don't bother with car
rentals as much as I used to.

00:15:47.270 --> 00:15:50.880
How many of you here have
shared a ride with a stranger?

00:15:55.610 --> 00:15:59.150
I'm pretty sure many of you
did not do this back in the day

00:15:59.150 --> 00:16:02.360
when you were
riding in taxicabs.

00:16:02.360 --> 00:16:04.850
It was not a very
common thing to get

00:16:04.850 --> 00:16:07.040
into a taxicab with a stranger.

00:16:07.040 --> 00:16:11.660
And yet, this is not a
millennial phenomena.

00:16:11.660 --> 00:16:13.250
Millennials are known to--

00:16:13.250 --> 00:16:15.120
or are expected to--

00:16:15.120 --> 00:16:17.210
are stereotyped with
putting their entire life

00:16:17.210 --> 00:16:18.350
on social media.

00:16:18.350 --> 00:16:21.350
And so we expect them to be very
comfortable with sharing rides

00:16:21.350 --> 00:16:22.310
with strangers.

00:16:22.310 --> 00:16:27.370
But clearly, we have a fairly
reasonable sample here,

00:16:27.370 --> 00:16:31.240
and in talking with drivers
as well as our partners

00:16:31.240 --> 00:16:33.640
across many countries, we
have found that it's not

00:16:33.640 --> 00:16:37.220
a millennial phenomena.

00:16:37.220 --> 00:16:39.280
How about commuting to
work using a ride sharing

00:16:39.280 --> 00:16:39.990
service, anyone?

00:16:42.830 --> 00:16:46.520
This is relatively less
common at the moment.

00:16:46.520 --> 00:16:48.140
I also know a few
of my colleagues

00:16:48.140 --> 00:16:51.960
at Google who have
sold their second cars

00:16:51.960 --> 00:16:55.120
and are using ride sharing
exclusively to get to work.

00:17:00.930 --> 00:17:05.810
Our habits-- our habits
dictated the principles

00:17:05.810 --> 00:17:11.141
by which we assign value
to products or services.

00:17:11.141 --> 00:17:12.599
Here's another
story that I'm going

00:17:12.599 --> 00:17:15.750
to tell you that many of you
road warriors will probably

00:17:15.750 --> 00:17:18.349
relate to.

00:17:18.349 --> 00:17:21.964
When I first got Wi-Fi on
a plane, I was like, wow,

00:17:21.964 --> 00:17:23.630
I don't know what to
do with this thing.

00:17:26.180 --> 00:17:28.970
I mean, my plane rides
were read a book,

00:17:28.970 --> 00:17:32.330
maybe listen to an audio book,
watch a movie, or just relax.

00:17:34.990 --> 00:17:37.480
And the first time I got
Wi-Fi, I was like, wow,

00:17:37.480 --> 00:17:41.110
I can have a communication,
real-time communication,

00:17:41.110 --> 00:17:44.920
with my family, with
my colleagues at work

00:17:44.920 --> 00:17:50.760
and impress them that I'm
still working at 35,000 feet.

00:17:50.760 --> 00:17:55.720
But now, if I'm on
a long-haul flight

00:17:55.720 --> 00:17:59.310
and I'm going to a
place for a meeting,

00:17:59.310 --> 00:18:03.530
I actually plan to
work during that time.

00:18:03.530 --> 00:18:05.440
I identify the parts
of the work that I'm

00:18:05.440 --> 00:18:08.760
going to finish when
I'm on the plane.

00:18:08.760 --> 00:18:11.850
And I see a few heads nodding.

00:18:11.850 --> 00:18:17.380
So if I don't get Wi-Fi, or if
the Wi-Fi is not fast enough

00:18:17.380 --> 00:18:20.140
to help me come through
an image-heavy slide deck,

00:18:20.140 --> 00:18:23.100
I'm really disappointed.

00:18:23.100 --> 00:18:25.920
So the value that I have
placed on that Wi-Fi

00:18:25.920 --> 00:18:29.010
is significantly different
now that my habits

00:18:29.010 --> 00:18:30.570
require a Wi-Fi on a plane.

00:18:35.730 --> 00:18:37.770
Now, while Moore's
law has kept up

00:18:37.770 --> 00:18:41.520
the advances in
technology-related products

00:18:41.520 --> 00:18:46.110
at an accelerating
pace, as consumers,

00:18:46.110 --> 00:18:52.030
our expectations and our
habits have not only adapted,

00:18:52.030 --> 00:18:54.480
but very quickly
exceeded the capability

00:18:54.480 --> 00:18:58.394
of technology very soon.

00:18:58.394 --> 00:18:59.685
Let me give you a few examples.

00:19:02.860 --> 00:19:09.170
20 years ago, our expectation
for on-demand, digital content

00:19:09.170 --> 00:19:14.210
was limited to getting access
to your short messages,

00:19:14.210 --> 00:19:17.420
your emails, anytime, anywhere.

00:19:17.420 --> 00:19:22.070
But today, we expect the
latest Hollywood blockbusters

00:19:22.070 --> 00:19:26.210
in ultra-high definition to be
accessible anytime, anywhere

00:19:26.210 --> 00:19:28.550
we are.

00:19:28.550 --> 00:19:32.300
What's more, now we
expect a real person

00:19:32.300 --> 00:19:35.000
to show up on our doorstep,
or wherever we are,

00:19:35.000 --> 00:19:38.730
within minutes of
summoning them.

00:19:38.730 --> 00:19:44.780
If you're hungry for something,
we expect freshly prepared food

00:19:44.780 --> 00:19:47.240
from our favorite
restaurant, even

00:19:47.240 --> 00:19:50.660
if it's a hole-in-the-wall,
to show up within an hour

00:19:50.660 --> 00:19:51.560
of desiring it.

00:19:54.610 --> 00:19:59.960
On the computational
side, it used to be,

00:19:59.960 --> 00:20:04.800
maybe 10, 12 years ago, that
we expected a supercomputer

00:20:04.800 --> 00:20:07.500
to be able to crunch through
lots of data, petabytes

00:20:07.500 --> 00:20:12.390
of data, and answer our
very structured queries

00:20:12.390 --> 00:20:15.060
within a fraction of a second.

00:20:15.060 --> 00:20:20.040
And today, we expect a device
that fits in our pockets,

00:20:20.040 --> 00:20:26.870
costs probably less than
$50, to understand us,

00:20:26.870 --> 00:20:31.820
to be able to predict our
needs even before we fully

00:20:31.820 --> 00:20:32.570
express them.

00:20:39.560 --> 00:20:42.140
We expect to have a
conversation with these devices.

00:20:42.140 --> 00:20:46.070
Yesterday, you saw during the
keynote an example of that.

00:20:46.070 --> 00:20:51.200
We expect them to
personalize their responses

00:20:51.200 --> 00:20:52.640
based on understanding us.

00:20:56.050 --> 00:20:59.140
And how much are we willing
to pay for all this?

00:20:59.140 --> 00:20:59.740
Not a lot.

00:21:02.420 --> 00:21:04.620
It started with digital content.

00:21:04.620 --> 00:21:09.430
We first got used to getting
unlimited digital content,

00:21:09.430 --> 00:21:13.870
whether it was unlimited
voice, unlimited data,

00:21:13.870 --> 00:21:19.510
unlimited music, unlimited
videos at a fixed price.

00:21:19.510 --> 00:21:23.540
And now we expect
physical services

00:21:23.540 --> 00:21:27.480
and unlimited quantities
at a fixed price.

00:21:27.480 --> 00:21:33.240
Unlimited shipping,
unlimited taxi rides--

00:21:33.240 --> 00:21:36.150
it's being tried out
in a few countries,

00:21:36.150 --> 00:21:38.810
the idea of unlimited taxi
rides for a fixed price.

00:21:41.530 --> 00:21:43.620
We used to put a huge
premium on personalization,

00:21:43.620 --> 00:21:45.810
on a personalized service.

00:21:45.810 --> 00:21:48.660
Today, your cab
driver addresses you

00:21:48.660 --> 00:21:52.290
by first name when
you meet him or her,

00:21:52.290 --> 00:21:56.760
or can personalize the
music to your liking

00:21:56.760 --> 00:21:59.100
as soon as you get in the car.

00:21:59.100 --> 00:22:00.440
And we just expect it.

00:22:05.680 --> 00:22:09.100
So my second point is that
the consumer principles

00:22:09.100 --> 00:22:14.350
dictate the economics
of these businesses.

00:22:14.350 --> 00:22:17.350
Price has always been
a very important driver

00:22:17.350 --> 00:22:19.080
of consumer adoption.

00:22:19.080 --> 00:22:21.400
And in the
ride-sharing space, we

00:22:21.400 --> 00:22:25.090
have seen that a
number of companies

00:22:25.090 --> 00:22:28.720
have innovated on low
cost product categories

00:22:28.720 --> 00:22:30.160
in order to drive adoption.

00:22:30.160 --> 00:22:34.210
And they have actually
seen very good results

00:22:34.210 --> 00:22:35.930
with lower cost categories.

00:22:38.600 --> 00:22:41.810
Delivery providers also
have seen higher adoption

00:22:41.810 --> 00:22:44.600
with lower cost deliveries.

00:22:44.600 --> 00:22:47.990
So it should come as
no surprise to you

00:22:47.990 --> 00:22:51.230
that user expectations
from these services

00:22:51.230 --> 00:22:54.920
and their willingness to pay are
headed in opposite directions.

00:22:58.970 --> 00:23:01.600
Now, even as
consumers' willingness

00:23:01.600 --> 00:23:05.590
to pay for these
services is reducing,

00:23:05.590 --> 00:23:09.160
the companies who are
enabling these experiences

00:23:09.160 --> 00:23:11.050
have to make enough
money so that when

00:23:11.050 --> 00:23:14.900
the venture capital dries out,
they can sustain the business.

00:23:18.430 --> 00:23:21.550
The suppliers of services in
this economy, the drivers,

00:23:21.550 --> 00:23:24.770
the full-time drivers or
the part-time drivers,

00:23:24.770 --> 00:23:28.250
they will make less as
a result on a per ride

00:23:28.250 --> 00:23:30.200
basis, on a per mile basis.

00:23:30.200 --> 00:23:34.010
But overall, they will make more
because of higher utilization

00:23:34.010 --> 00:23:40.625
enabled by efficiencies
that the system brings.

00:23:44.470 --> 00:23:46.210
But I digressed.

00:23:46.210 --> 00:23:49.750
Let me come back to user
experience for a moment.

00:23:49.750 --> 00:23:52.000
A few months ago,
my family and I

00:23:52.000 --> 00:23:54.430
were returning from a vacation.

00:23:54.430 --> 00:23:57.610
We landed in San Francisco
airport around midnight.

00:23:57.610 --> 00:23:58.990
It was cold.

00:23:58.990 --> 00:24:01.070
Our kids were with us.

00:24:01.070 --> 00:24:04.490
And we waited to order
our ride until after we

00:24:04.490 --> 00:24:06.320
had picked our
bags because there

00:24:06.320 --> 00:24:09.080
was some uncertainty in how
long it would take for our bags

00:24:09.080 --> 00:24:11.010
to arrive.

00:24:11.010 --> 00:24:15.330
Once we ordered our ride,
it took eight minutes.

00:24:15.330 --> 00:24:17.550
And we were there in the
cold, on the curbside,

00:24:17.550 --> 00:24:22.270
waiting for our car to arrive
for about eight minutes.

00:24:22.270 --> 00:24:24.520
Not a big deal, possibly.

00:24:24.520 --> 00:24:26.050
But we're not the only ones.

00:24:26.050 --> 00:24:30.860
We had other co-passengers
who were in a similar boat.

00:24:30.860 --> 00:24:33.100
It was around
midnight, after all.

00:24:33.100 --> 00:24:37.030
When we got in the car,
I asked the driver,

00:24:37.030 --> 00:24:40.000
and he said he too was
waiting in the parking lot

00:24:40.000 --> 00:24:41.890
at the airport,
or in the waiting

00:24:41.890 --> 00:24:48.790
lot, for several minutes before
he got a call for the ride.

00:24:48.790 --> 00:24:52.210
I know we have the ability
today to predict traffic

00:24:52.210 --> 00:24:56.680
in future based on
learnings of the patterns

00:24:56.680 --> 00:24:59.387
through machine learning,
big data, cloud computing, et

00:24:59.387 --> 00:25:00.310
cetera.

00:25:00.310 --> 00:25:02.440
And I know it is
very hard to predict

00:25:02.440 --> 00:25:07.700
when a given passenger who
walks out of the airplane

00:25:07.700 --> 00:25:10.950
will emerge on the curbside.

00:25:10.950 --> 00:25:13.530
But I'm sure there are
large opportunities here

00:25:13.530 --> 00:25:17.580
to reduce that wait time
both for the passengers

00:25:17.580 --> 00:25:19.770
and for the drivers.

00:25:19.770 --> 00:25:21.960
And it's not only
about user experience.

00:25:24.670 --> 00:25:27.510
It affects the bottom line.

00:25:27.510 --> 00:25:34.590
Let's imagine that a driver
saves two minutes on average

00:25:34.590 --> 00:25:38.290
on a typical, average
ride of 20 minutes.

00:25:38.290 --> 00:25:44.930
In an eight-hour day, he would
deliver 2.2 additional rides

00:25:44.930 --> 00:25:46.850
by shaving off just
a couple of minutes

00:25:46.850 --> 00:25:49.890
per ride on a daily basis.

00:25:49.890 --> 00:25:53.760
That means a 10% increase
in net pay for the driver.

00:25:53.760 --> 00:25:57.080
And that 10% goes straight
to the bottom line

00:25:57.080 --> 00:26:00.510
of the ride-sharing companies
because the marginal cost

00:26:00.510 --> 00:26:01.890
of that service is very minimal.

00:26:05.470 --> 00:26:09.330
This brings us back to
the question of economics.

00:26:09.330 --> 00:26:12.750
As consumers' willingness
to pay is going down

00:26:12.750 --> 00:26:15.510
for these services, and their
expectations are increasing,

00:26:15.510 --> 00:26:18.240
what levers do ride-sharing
and delivery companies

00:26:18.240 --> 00:26:24.050
have to increase or to
improve their economics?

00:26:24.050 --> 00:26:27.320
And we think that the answer
is efficiency at scale.

00:26:30.370 --> 00:26:35.970
So efficiency is key to
delivering the economics that

00:26:35.970 --> 00:26:39.900
is needed by ride-sharing
and delivery companies

00:26:39.900 --> 00:26:45.660
in order to satisfy the consumer
principles, the expectations,

00:26:45.660 --> 00:26:50.640
the behaviors that
customers expect because

00:26:50.640 --> 00:26:53.970
of the new habits that
they have developed

00:26:53.970 --> 00:26:58.184
as a result of these
location-powered ride-sharing

00:26:58.184 --> 00:26:59.100
and delivery services.

00:27:03.480 --> 00:27:07.710
Now let's examine some of
the key drivers of efficiency

00:27:07.710 --> 00:27:08.410
in this space.

00:27:12.320 --> 00:27:16.340
We're all familiar with
the notion of last mile.

00:27:16.340 --> 00:27:18.740
Last mile in the
delivery space typically

00:27:18.740 --> 00:27:24.020
refers to taking the goods
from warehouse to your home

00:27:24.020 --> 00:27:24.770
and delivering it.

00:27:28.899 --> 00:27:30.940
Given the prevalence of
ride-sharing and delivery

00:27:30.940 --> 00:27:35.500
companies around the world, over
600 countries around the world,

00:27:35.500 --> 00:27:38.320
it should be fairly obvious
that the efficiency bottleneck

00:27:38.320 --> 00:27:41.290
is not about the
last mile anymore.

00:27:41.290 --> 00:27:46.260
It has shifted to the last
1/10 of a mile, what I

00:27:46.260 --> 00:27:48.000
would call the last deci-mile.

00:27:50.399 --> 00:27:52.190
Let me illustrate this
with a few examples.

00:27:55.040 --> 00:27:58.710
When you order your
ride, the driver usually

00:27:58.710 --> 00:28:02.700
has no trouble getting
to within 1/10 of a mile

00:28:02.700 --> 00:28:05.690
from where you are.

00:28:05.690 --> 00:28:08.720
But typically, getting to
exactly where you are often

00:28:08.720 --> 00:28:13.740
requires a phone call or
more than one phone call.

00:28:13.740 --> 00:28:15.420
And if you're in
a foreign country,

00:28:15.420 --> 00:28:18.060
that phone call could be
very expensive both for you

00:28:18.060 --> 00:28:18.890
and for the driver.

00:28:22.750 --> 00:28:24.550
Because we haven't
quite perfected

00:28:24.550 --> 00:28:28.060
all the details
required to make sure

00:28:28.060 --> 00:28:32.880
that the driver arrives
exactly where you are.

00:28:32.880 --> 00:28:38.040
If you are here in Moscone
Center and you order a ride,

00:28:38.040 --> 00:28:40.380
Moscone Center
has Howard Street,

00:28:40.380 --> 00:28:42.420
which is a one-way on one end.

00:28:42.420 --> 00:28:44.100
Oh, by the way, this
is Moscone West,

00:28:44.100 --> 00:28:47.100
so you have to know that this
is Moscone West, and not Moscone

00:28:47.100 --> 00:28:48.930
North, or Moscone South.

00:28:48.930 --> 00:28:50.700
But then there are
two one-way streets.

00:28:50.700 --> 00:28:53.940
And it has doors on both sides.

00:28:53.940 --> 00:28:57.330
It's not sufficient to have your
driver come to Moscone Center.

00:28:57.330 --> 00:28:59.700
They need to know
exactly which route

00:28:59.700 --> 00:29:02.730
to take so they are on the
right side of the one-way street

00:29:02.730 --> 00:29:07.150
in order to arrive at
the exit where you are.

00:29:07.150 --> 00:29:08.850
Now, this can be
extremely frustrating

00:29:08.850 --> 00:29:11.590
if you are in a new
place as a rider

00:29:11.590 --> 00:29:14.360
and you don't know yourself
exactly where you are,

00:29:14.360 --> 00:29:17.590
let alone explain
that to a driver.

00:29:17.590 --> 00:29:19.709
And if you don't speak
the driver's language--

00:29:19.709 --> 00:29:22.000
it has happened to me a couple
of times, once in Japan,

00:29:22.000 --> 00:29:24.100
once in Latin America--

00:29:24.100 --> 00:29:25.864
that can be even
more frustrating.

00:29:28.470 --> 00:29:33.120
And how exactly does
the driver get to you?

00:29:33.120 --> 00:29:34.949
Has anyone had an
experience where

00:29:34.949 --> 00:29:36.990
you could see the building
where you needed to go

00:29:36.990 --> 00:29:40.850
but just couldn't
find how to get there?

00:29:40.850 --> 00:29:43.610
And you went in circles
trying to figure out the entry

00:29:43.610 --> 00:29:44.700
point to the building?

00:29:47.590 --> 00:29:51.610
Recently, a colleague
of mine was in the city.

00:29:51.610 --> 00:29:56.220
And she ordered a
ride and happened

00:29:56.220 --> 00:29:57.660
to be standing at a bus stop.

00:30:00.670 --> 00:30:03.700
The car pulled over,
she got into the car,

00:30:03.700 --> 00:30:07.120
and noticed there was a bus
coming right behind her.

00:30:07.120 --> 00:30:12.020
And the bus had a camera in the
front, and she was intrigued.

00:30:12.020 --> 00:30:15.770
Later, she found out, when she
had another co-passenger join

00:30:15.770 --> 00:30:22.530
her for that ride share, she
learned that the buses now

00:30:22.530 --> 00:30:25.680
have cameras so
that they can record

00:30:25.680 --> 00:30:30.515
which cars are parked at bus
stops or stop at bus stops.

00:30:30.515 --> 00:30:33.140
So the driver would probably be
expecting a ticket in the mail.

00:30:33.140 --> 00:30:35.700
Ouch.

00:30:35.700 --> 00:30:38.040
Of course, if you're
a regular taxi driver,

00:30:38.040 --> 00:30:41.070
you would probably
know these rules.

00:30:41.070 --> 00:30:43.699
But if you're a regular
Joe doing this part-time,

00:30:43.699 --> 00:30:45.990
it's very unlikely that you
would know all these rules.

00:30:51.320 --> 00:30:53.750
How long exactly will
it take to get there?

00:30:53.750 --> 00:30:57.860
When you order your car,
or you order your food

00:30:57.860 --> 00:31:01.500
to be delivered, the
company has to look

00:31:01.500 --> 00:31:06.180
at the supply, the suppliers,
the drivers who are around you,

00:31:06.180 --> 00:31:11.310
and find the driver who's
closest to you for whom

00:31:11.310 --> 00:31:14.400
it is most efficient to get
to you as soon as possible,

00:31:14.400 --> 00:31:18.990
which requires very
precise prediction of how

00:31:18.990 --> 00:31:21.180
long the driver will
take to get to you.

00:31:21.180 --> 00:31:23.340
Because not having
that answer correctly

00:31:23.340 --> 00:31:25.410
could mean longer
wait time for you

00:31:25.410 --> 00:31:30.190
as a passenger and probably
a suboptimal job allocation

00:31:30.190 --> 00:31:30.940
to a driver.

00:31:30.940 --> 00:31:33.130
Perhaps another
driver could have

00:31:33.130 --> 00:31:37.970
done that job faster and
probably yielded a higher

00:31:37.970 --> 00:31:41.300
return overall.

00:31:41.300 --> 00:31:45.790
So knowing exactly how long it
takes to get to a pick-up point

00:31:45.790 --> 00:31:49.000
makes a huge impact
to the bottom line

00:31:49.000 --> 00:31:54.600
once you aggregate it over
millions and millions of rides

00:31:54.600 --> 00:31:55.120
every day.

00:31:57.930 --> 00:32:00.890
Even as a consumer we
don't like to wait.

00:32:00.890 --> 00:32:04.640
And you're probably all familiar
with airlines and hotels who

00:32:04.640 --> 00:32:07.640
have increased the
value of less wait time

00:32:07.640 --> 00:32:11.090
by giving their premier
customers special lanes

00:32:11.090 --> 00:32:14.160
to get into the airplane fast,
even though the airplane is not

00:32:14.160 --> 00:32:19.730
going to take off any faster,
or check in faster at the hotel.

00:32:23.760 --> 00:32:27.240
Riders today expect
to be picked up

00:32:27.240 --> 00:32:31.040
where they are even if
that point is not exactly

00:32:31.040 --> 00:32:32.460
on the map.

00:32:32.460 --> 00:32:35.930
If you are in a
big shopping mall,

00:32:35.930 --> 00:32:40.000
and you happen to exit out
of the gate, a small gate

00:32:40.000 --> 00:32:43.945
at the back of the mall that
is flanked by two large stores,

00:32:43.945 --> 00:32:46.150
so it's hardly even
visible on the map,

00:32:46.150 --> 00:32:50.010
that's where to expect
the driver to arrive.

00:32:50.010 --> 00:32:51.684
If you're in a large
office complex,

00:32:51.684 --> 00:32:53.100
you want the driver
to pick you up

00:32:53.100 --> 00:32:56.450
right outside as you walk
outside your office building.

00:32:59.210 --> 00:33:01.310
If you want food to
be delivered to you,

00:33:01.310 --> 00:33:03.170
you want it right
outside your apartment

00:33:03.170 --> 00:33:05.870
even if that means the
driver has to figure out

00:33:05.870 --> 00:33:09.260
where exactly to park
and how exactly to walk

00:33:09.260 --> 00:33:11.510
from there to the
place where you expect

00:33:11.510 --> 00:33:14.110
the food to be delivered.

00:33:14.110 --> 00:33:17.050
Sure, we can drop
a point on a map.

00:33:17.050 --> 00:33:19.600
We can do that very effectively.

00:33:19.600 --> 00:33:23.560
Drop a pin on the map and
assume it'll all just work out.

00:33:23.560 --> 00:33:26.650
But dropping a pin
on a map gives you

00:33:26.650 --> 00:33:28.960
a latitude and a
longitude, which

00:33:28.960 --> 00:33:33.060
is very hard for
us humans to parse.

00:33:33.060 --> 00:33:35.730
So the example of Moscone
Center, just dropping

00:33:35.730 --> 00:33:38.520
a latitude and longitude is
not sufficient for the driver

00:33:38.520 --> 00:33:42.600
to know that you are
on the Howard Street

00:33:42.600 --> 00:33:47.070
side of the Moscone Center
and you're waiting right

00:33:47.070 --> 00:33:49.110
outside the middle entrance.

00:33:49.110 --> 00:33:51.870
So converting that
information to something

00:33:51.870 --> 00:33:58.830
that human beings can understand
requires a vast knowledge

00:33:58.830 --> 00:34:01.640
at a granularity that
is finer than what

00:34:01.640 --> 00:34:02.803
we have developed today.

00:34:07.540 --> 00:34:12.130
And while it is helpful to
know where every place is,

00:34:12.130 --> 00:34:15.320
even if it is a
smaller place, you

00:34:15.320 --> 00:34:18.860
want to know is that place--
if you're going someplace,

00:34:18.860 --> 00:34:22.190
say you're going to a
bar with your friends.

00:34:22.190 --> 00:34:26.030
Before you go there, you want to
make sure that the place still

00:34:26.030 --> 00:34:31.460
exists, it is still there, that
it is open, that it is busy.

00:34:31.460 --> 00:34:33.650
Nobody wants to go to
a bar that is not busy.

00:34:33.650 --> 00:34:36.739
But it's not too
busy that you can't

00:34:36.739 --> 00:34:39.469
make your way to the
bartender for 15 minutes.

00:34:42.380 --> 00:34:44.210
And of course, is
it worth going?

00:34:44.210 --> 00:34:47.080
Is that place worth going?

00:34:47.080 --> 00:34:50.040
There's a lot of
information about location

00:34:50.040 --> 00:34:53.010
that is needed at a scale--

00:34:53.010 --> 00:34:56.400
at a global scale--

00:34:56.400 --> 00:35:02.110
in order to support
the economy, the space.

00:35:05.770 --> 00:35:07.510
The real world
isn't static either.

00:35:07.510 --> 00:35:10.480
It changes from one
second to the next.

00:35:10.480 --> 00:35:12.640
And we have done
a phenomenal job

00:35:12.640 --> 00:35:18.240
in knowing the traffic patterns
with real-time traffic.

00:35:18.240 --> 00:35:22.120
But there's a lot
that needs to be done.

00:35:22.120 --> 00:35:26.070
As the flow of businesses,
as new businesses open, flow

00:35:26.070 --> 00:35:28.750
of people to businesses
change, there's

00:35:28.750 --> 00:35:30.550
a lot of this
information that is

00:35:30.550 --> 00:35:33.460
essential to making
the right decisions

00:35:33.460 --> 00:35:34.610
in an efficient manner.

00:35:37.190 --> 00:35:39.770
Has anyone ever
experienced a situation

00:35:39.770 --> 00:35:47.160
where they found that a road
that was recommended by the map

00:35:47.160 --> 00:35:50.670
took you through a barricade
because the road was barricaded

00:35:50.670 --> 00:35:53.100
off for some event?

00:35:53.100 --> 00:35:55.950
And we thought, oh, there's
no traffic here, probably

00:35:55.950 --> 00:35:59.880
the fastest way to get across.

00:35:59.880 --> 00:36:04.210
Or a road that has had been
washed away by the storm

00:36:04.210 --> 00:36:06.110
the previous night?

00:36:06.110 --> 00:36:10.490
So there's a lot of real-time
changes, real-time events that

00:36:10.490 --> 00:36:17.450
are happening the world over,
and knowing them precisely

00:36:17.450 --> 00:36:22.350
is important for the
efficiency that is required

00:36:22.350 --> 00:36:29.120
for these companies to thrive.

00:36:29.120 --> 00:36:32.430
So we are really excited by the
opportunities, the challenges

00:36:32.430 --> 00:36:40.560
and the opportunities that are
presented in order to make it

00:36:40.560 --> 00:36:46.760
seamless and
delightful regardless

00:36:46.760 --> 00:36:49.010
of the circumstances.

00:36:49.010 --> 00:36:51.440
So there are three key
pillars of efficiency

00:36:51.440 --> 00:36:53.590
that I've discussed.

00:36:53.590 --> 00:36:58.160
The last deci-mile efficiency,
knowing the places,

00:36:58.160 --> 00:37:00.410
knowing every
place on the planet

00:37:00.410 --> 00:37:03.920
at a granularity that is
much finer than before.

00:37:03.920 --> 00:37:08.970
And we've done a tremendous
job of indexing places

00:37:08.970 --> 00:37:11.040
the world over.

00:37:11.040 --> 00:37:15.640
And while what we have
is not comprehensive,

00:37:15.640 --> 00:37:18.000
it is probably the
best that exists.

00:37:18.000 --> 00:37:22.570
And yet, we need to
go one level deeper.

00:37:22.570 --> 00:37:25.030
We not only need to go
broader to have more coverage,

00:37:25.030 --> 00:37:27.386
but we also need to
go one level deeper

00:37:27.386 --> 00:37:30.010
to get to that exactness, to the
preciseness that I was talking

00:37:30.010 --> 00:37:35.030
about, and make sure that all
of the real-time information

00:37:35.030 --> 00:37:40.520
is even more enriched with
information about places,

00:37:40.520 --> 00:37:42.410
about roads, and so forth.

00:37:42.410 --> 00:37:44.780
So we are really excited
by the opportunities

00:37:44.780 --> 00:37:46.280
that this presents
going forward.

00:37:49.710 --> 00:37:51.970
Let me leave you with a few
thoughts about the future.

00:37:55.550 --> 00:37:58.190
As we tackle the
problems today in order

00:37:58.190 --> 00:38:02.970
to make the on-demand
economy thrive,

00:38:02.970 --> 00:38:06.060
where do we see this going
forward in the future?

00:38:10.290 --> 00:38:16.540
Imagine if you could predict
how many people were going

00:38:16.540 --> 00:38:22.340
to show up at the Yankees
baseball game that is happening

00:38:22.340 --> 00:38:27.650
in a few hours, or maybe in a
few days, and how many of them

00:38:27.650 --> 00:38:32.050
would need a ride-sharing
service to get to that place,

00:38:32.050 --> 00:38:35.830
or how many of them would need
a ride-share back and from which

00:38:35.830 --> 00:38:37.870
locations.

00:38:37.870 --> 00:38:42.280
It is easy-- we heard
the example of Pokemon Go

00:38:42.280 --> 00:38:45.820
where the demand
they expected was

00:38:45.820 --> 00:38:49.540
significantly lower than the
actual demand they observed.

00:38:49.540 --> 00:38:53.800
It was 50x the demand they
expected in the best case.

00:38:53.800 --> 00:38:56.890
And with Google Cloud, we were
able to very quickly provision

00:38:56.890 --> 00:38:59.230
them as we saw that demand.

00:38:59.230 --> 00:39:03.370
Because provisioning hardware
and software is something

00:39:03.370 --> 00:39:07.380
we have really
nailed with Cloud.

00:39:07.380 --> 00:39:12.540
But getting real people
in their cars provisioned

00:39:12.540 --> 00:39:17.680
to satisfy demand, there's
some physics involved there.

00:39:17.680 --> 00:39:18.980
And that's hard.

00:39:18.980 --> 00:39:22.440
So the sooner you can
predict, the more efficient

00:39:22.440 --> 00:39:26.610
you will be in order
to capture that demand.

00:39:26.610 --> 00:39:33.220
Imagine being able to predict
after a Beyonce concert in Los

00:39:33.220 --> 00:39:36.460
Angeles how many people,
once they head home

00:39:36.460 --> 00:39:40.840
after the concert at 9:00 PM,
are going to request delivery

00:39:40.840 --> 00:39:46.650
of food, and being able to
provision it in such a way

00:39:46.650 --> 00:39:50.760
that you can deliver the food to
them within 30 minutes of them

00:39:50.760 --> 00:39:53.640
desiring it, and
knowing which restaurant

00:39:53.640 --> 00:39:56.580
and what cuisines
they would like

00:39:56.580 --> 00:40:04.110
so that the restaurants are also
equipped to handle the load.

00:40:04.110 --> 00:40:08.020
These are some of the questions
our partners and our customers

00:40:08.020 --> 00:40:10.800
are asking us to help with.

00:40:10.800 --> 00:40:17.000
And we are thrilled
at the opportunities

00:40:17.000 --> 00:40:19.400
and the challenges
that it presents.

00:40:19.400 --> 00:40:21.900
With our knowledge
of the real world

00:40:21.900 --> 00:40:25.340
and behavioral insights
into how people behave

00:40:25.340 --> 00:40:29.290
in the real world, we
believe we are very well

00:40:29.290 --> 00:40:32.374
situated to address some
of these challenges.

00:40:38.560 --> 00:40:41.110
Where does that leave
us with another trend

00:40:41.110 --> 00:40:42.850
that we see in the future?

00:40:42.850 --> 00:40:47.850
How many of you here
would be shocked

00:40:47.850 --> 00:40:51.410
if self-driving
cars were providing

00:40:51.410 --> 00:40:57.771
ride-sharing services,
say, by 2020?

00:40:57.771 --> 00:40:58.270
OK.

00:40:58.270 --> 00:41:00.190
A few of you.

00:41:00.190 --> 00:41:02.060
So maybe not 2020.

00:41:02.060 --> 00:41:05.910
Maybe 3, 4, 5 years.

00:41:05.910 --> 00:41:08.280
I feel I'm in fairly good
company here that most of you

00:41:08.280 --> 00:41:12.140
think that you would
not be shocked.

00:41:12.140 --> 00:41:15.930
The question that
I wondered about

00:41:15.930 --> 00:41:20.640
was the problems, the
challenges of economics

00:41:20.640 --> 00:41:22.930
that I mentioned earlier.

00:41:22.930 --> 00:41:29.630
Does it still apply in the
world where there's no driver?

00:41:29.630 --> 00:41:33.380
That it's all self-driving cars,
maybe self-driving vehicles

00:41:33.380 --> 00:41:37.150
to deliver food to your
place, or packages,

00:41:37.150 --> 00:41:41.140
or drones that can
deliver in your backyard,

00:41:41.140 --> 00:41:48.970
in your driveway, or right
in front of your front door?

00:41:48.970 --> 00:41:51.190
How does some of the
challenges that we see today

00:41:51.190 --> 00:41:54.100
apply in that future which
is not very far away?

00:41:59.280 --> 00:42:07.110
If, with five senses, and
the human intelligence,

00:42:07.110 --> 00:42:11.670
and a smartphone that can
harness the power of the cloud,

00:42:11.670 --> 00:42:15.430
and the real-time
knowledge of the world

00:42:15.430 --> 00:42:18.250
human beings still
have problems solving

00:42:18.250 --> 00:42:23.300
some of these on
a daily basis, it

00:42:23.300 --> 00:42:29.190
would be my guess that
for a robot to solve

00:42:29.190 --> 00:42:35.580
them would require more not less
data, more not less services,

00:42:35.580 --> 00:42:37.710
more not less information.

00:42:37.710 --> 00:42:39.840
Some of the things
that we're doing today,

00:42:39.840 --> 00:42:41.910
we're building a
bridge to the future.

00:42:41.910 --> 00:42:45.750
If this future were to arrive
where self-driving cars would

00:42:45.750 --> 00:42:48.780
show up where you
want them to show up,

00:42:48.780 --> 00:42:52.750
when you want them
to show up, you

00:42:52.750 --> 00:42:56.560
would need all the services and
the exactness and the precision

00:42:56.560 --> 00:43:00.530
that I mentioned in
order for that to happen.

00:43:00.530 --> 00:43:03.530
So we're really excited,
as we look into the future,

00:43:03.530 --> 00:43:08.487
we're really excited
by those opportunities.

00:43:08.487 --> 00:43:09.320
Thank you very much.

00:43:09.320 --> 00:43:10.520
[APPLAUSE]

00:43:11.720 --> 00:43:14.170
[MUSIC PLAYING]

